WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.900
This video is sponsored by the Great Courses
Plus.

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:09.299
It's no secret that Albert Einstein worked
at the Swiss Patent Office after graduating

00:00:09.299 --> 00:00:10.889
from his university studies.

00:00:10.889 --> 00:00:17.330
He had really wanted a job in physics, teaching
or researching, but his university professors

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:21.660
wouldn't really cooperate and wouldn't give
him good recommendations.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:27.019
He'd had reasonably good grades, but had a
few falling outs with professors he didn't

00:00:27.019 --> 00:00:28.019
like.

00:00:28.019 --> 00:00:33.610
Eventually, it was through his friend's dad,
that Einstein got the job of examining patent

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:34.610
applications.

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:40.579
It was a matter of looking through various
mechanical inventions, and assessing their

00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:47.590
practicality and originality to see if the
inventor should be granted rights over the

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:48.590
idea.

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:54.109
It does seem an intriguing thought, to wonder
how his time in this job may have influenced

00:00:54.109 --> 00:00:55.449
Einstein's later work.

00:00:55.449 --> 00:01:02.540
So out of curiosity, I've tracked down some
of the actual patents that Einstein approved.

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:09.180
Einstein worked at the patent office from
1902 to 1909, which spans the period of time

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:14.300
when Einstein published his groundbreaking
papers and began to gain recognition.

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:20.460
So it doesn't seem unreasonable that this
work provided at least some inspiration.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.980
However, not all of the inventions were glamorous.

00:01:23.980 --> 00:01:26.120
Let's take a look at this first one.

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:31.460
It's a weather indicator based on air humidity,
it's written in German but translates to say

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:36.730
that we have the device based on a twisted
string of animal intestine, that's labelled

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:43.140
with the a in the diagram here, that reacts
to the moisture content of the air, and changes

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:49.560
in humidity caused the intestine to twist,
which moves the needle in the display.

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:53.400
Einstein himself looked at this and thought
'seems legit'.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:56.000
And actually, the concept has been used a
lot.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.810
For example, in hygrometers, or weather houses.

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:02.830
The intestine is called a catgut.

00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:05.260
But it is not made from cats.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:08.310
Rather, it's made from sheep, goat or cattle.

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:15.760
This next pattern was for a gravel sorting
machine, a series of various sized wire mesh

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:22.130
cylinders are rotated using the handle, with
gravel coming in through this side, small

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:28.200
gravel will fall through the fine mesh with
larger gravel making its way to the large

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:31.450
mesh, and that sorts the gravel.

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:38.440
Patents are practical engineering based solutions,
rather than the deeply theoretical and abstract

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:41.960
work that Einstein would later become famous
for.

00:02:41.960 --> 00:02:45.050
Here's another one that Einstein approved.

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:48.800
It's for a kind of electrical typewriter.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:54.130
With this, the keys are still pressed by a
person, but the metal lever which imprints

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:58.290
the paper now moves electrically, rather than
mechanically.

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:02.340
And this is some detail regarding that process.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:08.420
There's a bit of a conspiracy swirling around
that Einstein stole some of his greatest ideas

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:11.050
from the patents that he read.

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:13.770
There are obvious practical challenges to
pulling that off.

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:20.180
But it also doesn't make a lot of sense, given
that patent applications are not the same

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:21.470
as scientific papers.

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:28.190
They don't present new theoretical ideas,
but rather the commercial applications.

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:33.890
But it does seem likely to me that having
access to cutting edge inventions, and having

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:39.470
to think deeply about the ideas behind them
certainly would have helped, especially when

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:45.670
Einstein was able to read patents surrounding
ideas in electromagnetism, transmission of

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:49.830
signals and even the synchronisation of time.

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:55.270
Here is one that Einstein reviewed that was
submitted by the General Electric Company,

00:03:55.270 --> 00:04:02.110
an AC commutators with short circuit brushes
and opposite brush auxiliary coils to avoid

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:03.110
sparks.

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:08.920
It's always good to try and minimise the amount
of cables going to or from a machine.

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:15.569
So here they are using anchor points of the
machine as a way to connect the commutator

00:04:15.569 --> 00:04:16.569
coil.

00:04:16.569 --> 00:04:21.910
But what's really interesting is that it includes
one of the only surviving notes written by

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:25.370
Einstein while he was working at the office.

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:32.070
It's a sort of rejection letter, saying that
the patent claim is incorrect, imprecise and

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:34.770
not clearly formulated.

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:39.630
Einstein's boss at the patent office had in
fact told Einstein that "when you pick up

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:44.770
an application, you should think that anything
the inventor says is wrong".

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:52.220
It would seem that in his earlier conflict
with authority whilst a student Einstein already

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:58.160
had a bit of this mindset, but this attitude
to work must have helped him to forge new

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:02.090
ground by not being limited to existing beliefs.

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:08.090
Einstein then got promoted to technical expert
at a time when the office was seing a growing

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:14.260
number of patents regarding electric timekeeping
and synchronisation of clocks.

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:20.010
This application likely came across his desk
for an electromagnetically controlled pendulum

00:05:20.010 --> 00:05:24.560
that would take a signal and correct a distant
pendulum clock.

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:30.590
Many people were thinking about clocks, not
as thought experiments, but because keeping

00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:34.310
clocks synchronised was a real problem.

00:05:34.310 --> 00:05:40.540
Patents were being filed trying to help determine
train arrival times using coordinated clocks.

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:47.430
But it was Einstein who made a jump to realising
there was an inseparable relation between

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:49.740
time and space.

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:56.660
In 1905, he published his work on special
relativity, as well as the photoelectric effect,

00:05:56.660 --> 00:06:00.730
Brownian motion and the energy mass equivalence.

00:06:00.730 --> 00:06:06.570
Long after he left the patent office, he was
still developing ideas based on current gaps

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:13.020
in physics, and gaining plenty of his own
Patents, including his ideas for an adjustable

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:14.020
blouse.

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:18.660
But not all of Einstein's ideas were great
or even correct.

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:24.110
And I think it's important to remember that
even people who have been elevated to genius

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:27.550
status, have made mistakes or have failed.

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:32.900
If you would like to hear more about Einstein's
flaws, then I know just the thing for you.

